From 870f6e8220988523751fa94c6b428d510836b1d7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: julian <julian@jc84.com>
Date: Thu, 11 Jun 2026 09:44:36 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 02/11] feat: deferred tap-to-focus via focus-on-touch
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Add `input { touchscreen { focus-on-touch "touch-down" | "touch-up" } }`.

With "touch-down" (default, current behavior) the window under a touch
is activated the moment the first finger lands. On a touchscreen the
first finger of a multi-finger gesture therefore focuses — and scrolls
the view to fit — whatever window it happens to land on, before the
recognizer has any chance to claim the sequence as a gesture. The
client-side touch is already taken back via wl_touch.cancel at the
gesture transition, but the compositor-side focus change was not.

With "touch-up", activation is recorded as pending at touch-down and
committed only when the last finger lifts. The gesture-claim transition
and touch-cancel clear the pending activation, so compositor gestures
never move focus. A plain tap focuses exactly as before, just on lift.

Mod+touch still activates immediately (the move grab needs the window
active, and Mod is an explicit request for a compositor action).

xdg-activation self-requests are routed through the same deferral:
Chromium-based browsers mint an activation token from the forwarded
touch-down serial and request self-activation, which would bypass the
pending mechanism. When the requested window is already pending
deferred tap-to-focus, the request is left to the pending commit
(focus on lift) instead of activating immediately.

Also scrub two stale IPC-progress doc mentions left over from the
gesture-IPC extraction.
